## 2. The synthetic world (generator)

`gnm.sim.generator` builds the world from `world_seed = 2023`.

**Fleet.** 24 narrowbody (A320-family archetype) and 16 widebody (787-family archetype) aircraft with fictional registrations (`A6-FH?`, `A6-FW?`). Daily utilisation is drawn per aircraft: {{RESULT:config.utilisation}} (ASSUMED). Weekly, monthly and holiday seasonality factors scale utilisation.

**Network.** Hub AUH and six outstations (LHR, FRA, CAI, JED, BOM, SIN). Station coordinates are REAL (OurAirports). Narrowbodies fly to CAI, JED and BOM; widebodies to LHR, FRA, BOM and SIN. BOM is the only outstation both fleets serve.

**Parts: a curated, type-exclusive catalogue** (V2-SIM-1). The 120 fictional part numbers (`GN-<ATA>-<C|E|R><nnn>`: 45 consumables, 35 expendables, 40 rotables) come from a hand-curated catalogue that fixes, per row, the ATA chapter, description, family, type applicability, quantity per aircraft, rectification window, removal mode and driver. Applicability is type-exclusive: only 12 common items (masks, lamps, battery packs, hardware, fluids and similar) fit both archetypes. The 787-family archetype has no ATA 36 bleed parts and gets electrical analogues instead. Unit cost is drawn log-uniform by family, and MTBUR, repair TAT and lead time are still drawn from the world seed within ASSUMED ranges.

**Reliability.** Removal-driven parts have a true MTBUR ({{RESULT:config.mtbur_ranges}}). The part master publishes a **vendor-quoted** MTBUR: {{RESULT:config.quote_noise}} ([D005](docs/DECISIONS.md), calibrated in V2-SIM-CAL). About 30% of parts have overdispersed, clustered demand. All of this is ASSUMED and SIMULATED. None of it is measured reliability.

**No-fault-found** (V2-SIM-4). A removal is NFF with probability = {{RESULT:config.nff}}. NFF shortens the repair pipeline, which matters for avionics.

**Wheels and brakes** (V2-SIM-3, V2-SIM-15). Wheel-and-tyre assemblies are {{RESULT:config.wheel_shop}}. Brakes are type-specific and wear-planned: their removals are known {{RESULT:config.wear_notice}} ahead and rectified at AUH (section 4).

**Planted situations.** Four documented demo situations (PS1–PS4) give the planner screens something real to find: PS1 {{RESULT:story.ps1}}, an unscheduled D0 rotable that the legacy rule does not stock at BOM; an overdue repair order at the EU vendor; a declining consumable creating excess; and a lead-time jump on one expendable around week 70.

**Scenarios.** `generate_scenario(world, truth, seed, week_from, week_to)` returns the exogenous events for one seed: flights, the maintenance schedule, removals and material requests, plus pre-sampled repair durations, condemnation and NFF flags, PO lead-time streams and the per-request uniforms the AOG routes use. Downstream code receives only this `Scenario`, never the truth.

