## 8. Inventory and rotable policy maths

### 8.1 Palm's theorem and the repair pipeline

Let removals arrive as a Poisson process with rate λ, and let each removed unit spend a turnaround time *T* in the pipeline (core return + transit + shop queue + shop work + return), with mean E[T]. **Palm's theorem** says the number of units in the pipeline at a random moment, *X*, is Poisson with mean λ · E[T], whatever the shape of the TAT distribution, provided repair times are independent and repair capacity is unlimited:

$$
X \sim \text{Poisson}(\lambda \cdot E[T])
$$

### 8.2 Base stock, backorders and fill

With total spares *S*, a demand is filled immediately if fewer than *S* units are in the pipeline:

$$
\text{EBO}(S) = \sum_{x > S} (x - S)\,P(X = x), \qquad \text{fill} = P(X \le S - 1)
$$

The protection level of every policy is defined on this fill, the quantity the engine measures as immediate fill (V2-SIM-10). EBO(S) − EBO(S+1) = P(X > S) falls as S rises, so each extra unit is worth less than the one before. A worked example is in the [study guide](STUDY_GUIDE.md). Rotable floats always use the Poisson pipeline; clustered removals make that optimistic, and the event engine (which simulates the clustering) is the referee.

### 8.3 Consumables and expendables: (s, S)

Lead-time demand (LTD) comes from the champion forecast over the planner's estimated lead time (recency-weighted history receipts with a supplier-change rule; this is what catches PS4), negative binomial when variance/mean ≥ 1.05, else Poisson. The reorder point *s* is the LTD quantile at the deadline-class target (D0 0.97, D3 0.92, D10 0.85, NONE 0.85; ASSUMED); *S* = *s* + EOQ, capped at 52 weeks of cover and half the shelf life.

### 8.4 Policies compared

| Policy | Description |
|---|---|
| **P0 Legacy OEM-quote** (V2-SIM-8) | A competent static baseline on quoted data only. Rotables: total spares S = smallest S with P(Poisson(quoted annual removals × quoted pipeline days ÷ 365) ≤ S − 1) ≥ {{RESULT:config.p0_protection}}, held at AUH, plus 1 line-station unit only for a D0 part with at least {{RESULT:config.p0_line_min}} quoted removals a year at that station. Consumables and expendables: {{RESULT:config.p0_ce}}, at every station that sees quoted demand. It holds {{RESULT:network.P0.outstation_rotables}} rotables at the outstations. |
| **P1 Local optimised** | Every stocking point sized on its own forecast demand (single-echelon): (s, S) for C/E, and a Palm float at each station for rotables. It holds {{RESULT:network.P1.outstation_rotables}} outstation rotables. A deliberate "every station for itself" comparator. |
| **P2 Network** | Hub float sized on the network pipeline; outstation *j* gets its *k*-th rotable unit only while the expected annual cost of a local miss avoided per AED of unit cost is at least the 20% carrying rate. The miss cost uses the v2 AOG routes (hub transfer with the hub's fill probability, otherwise local borrow then exchange/loan, or local buy), the hourly rate and the fixed cost per event (V2-POL-4). Laterals and rebalancing on. It holds {{RESULT:network.P2.outstation_rotables}} outstation rotables. |
| **I125** (frontier option) | P2 within the stocking guardrails, plus greedy additions up to 1.25 × P2's allocation capital ([section 10](#10-greedy-marginal-allocation-and-the-readiness-frontier)). |

All policies keep the AOG desk routes on. P0, P1 and P2 share one definition of total spares: the engine seeds each repair loop with units from P0's own plan ({{RESULT:policy.loop_units}} in total), and P1/P2 hand the engine Palm's S minus those loop units at the hub (V2-POL-3), so capital is comparable. Total rotable spares: P0 {{RESULT:policy.P0.rotable_total_spares}}, P1 {{RESULT:policy.P1.rotable_total_spares}}, P2 {{RESULT:policy.P2.rotable_total_spares}}, I125 {{RESULT:policy.I125.rotable_total_spares}}.

